sublimation. The physical transformation of a solid into a gas without intermediate melting

2
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:42,060
to the liquid is called sublimation. While this particular phase transformation only

3
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:47,720
occurs with solids that have appreciable vapor pressure below the melting point, in these

4
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:53,920
cases it can sometimes be used as a purification technique. This video will illustrate the

5
00:00:53,920 --> 00:01:00,240
atmospheric pressure sublimation of ferrocene. While sublimations can also be carried out

6
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:05,700
under reduced pressure, the basic principles and steps are the same, so a reduced pressure

7
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:12,320
sublimation will not be demonstrated. Sublimation can occur when a compound has appreciable

8
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:18,580
vapor pressure below its melting point. In certain cases, sublimation can be used as

9
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:26,300
a purification technique in the laboratory. It only works when a compound can undergo sublimation

10
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:33,380
and subsequent condensation without decomposition. It is also essential that none of the impurities

11
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:42,000
in the sample sublime under the same conditions. An atmospheric pressure sublimation setup

12
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,880
is very simple. It consists of a hot plate, the bottom of a culture dish containing the

13
00:01:49,880 --> 00:02:07,200
impure sample, the lid of the culture dish, and an ice bath. It's really that simple.

14
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,880
Start by transferring your impure sample to the culture dish. Spread it out so that

15
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:22,870
it is no more than 5 mm thick at any point. Place the sample on the hot plate and top

16
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:39,720
it with the lid. Set the ice bath on the lid and turn the heat on very low. Be patient.

17
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:51,280
Caution. Overheating will destroy your material. But it's a good way to make rotar.

18
00:02:51,280 --> 00:03:02,410
With a little patience and the right conditions, you will soon observe formation of crystals on

19
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:15,260
the lid of the culture dish. Let a layer of crystals grow and then turn off the heat. Let

20
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:20,180
this setup cool down a little bit before you gently slide the ice bath off of the lid.

21
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:34,160
Caution. If you're not careful, you'll lose your crystals.

22
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:50,690
Careful, the lid might stick to the bath, which can be disastrous. If you were careful enough,

23
00:03:50,690 --> 00:04:01,160
you can now lift the lid and see the layer of beautiful purified crystals. Scrape the crystals

24
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:06,920
into a clean watch glass and marvel over the dramatic improvement over your initial sample.

25
00:04:18,210 --> 00:04:23,090
I know it doesn't look like a lot of crystals, but remember, this is just the first batch.

26
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:33,670
Replace the lid on the culture dish, top it with the ice bath, and turn the heat back on

27
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:45,600
